---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import ExplanationList from "../../../components/story/ExplanationList.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, n, chronology, headlinesVsRule, steps, explanations } from "../../../data/ai-chip-exports-2026-11-16-commerce-requires-licenses-for-chips-at-china-serving-data-centers.mjs";
const story = "ai-chip-exports--2026-11-16-commerce-requires-licenses-for-chips-at-china-serving-data-centers";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short.replace(/^[A-Z][a-z]{2,3}\.? [0-9, ]*[0-9]{0,4}: /, "");
const cap = (s) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const timeline = chronology.map((e) => ({ date: e.date, title: cap(short(e.c)), current: e.current, cites: [{ c: e.c }] }));
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/ai-chip-exports/">AI chip exports</a><span>November 16, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">AI chip exports &middot; November 16, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact" data-claims="rule-issued data-center-license d5-includes-china abroad-only existing-90-days">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="existing-90-days" /><Cite c="installed-chips" /><Cite c="data-center-license" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-got-here">A rule promised for 18 months</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-wrote-it">Who wrote it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#explanations">Every explanation, tested</a></li>
          <li><a href="#coverage">Three reports got the start date or the rule's status wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">Shipping advanced U.S. AI chips to a data center abroad now needs a U.S. license when the shipper knows that data center rents large amounts of computing power to Chinese companies, under a rule the Commerce Department issued on November 16.<Cite c="rule-issued" /><Cite c="data-center-license" /><Cite c="d5-includes-china" /> The cutoff is {n.threshold} "aggregate TPP," a measure of the chips' combined processing power: about what {n.h100} of Nvidia's H100 chips deliver, by one estimate.<Cite c="data-center-license" /><Cite c="tpp-defined" /><Cite c="threshold-in-chips" /> Shipping these chips to Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand or Brazil now needs a license unless the end user is headquartered in the United States, or its parent company is, and the exporter reports the sale.<Cite c="five-countries" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">A U.S. license has been required to sell the most advanced AI chips to China since October 2022, and to sell them to Chinese-headquartered companies anywhere outside the United States since November 2023.<Cite c="direct-ban-2022" /><Cite c="may-31-tolerance" /> Chinese companies that could not buy the chips directly rented them from data centers in Southeast Asia instead.<Cite c="rent-abroad" /> Offshore subsidiaries and resellers can stand between a data center and the company using it.<Cite c="kharon-intermediaries" /> The rule counts a customer as Chinese if its ultimate parent company is headquartered in China, so renting through a subsidiary abroad still counts.<Cite c="data-center-license" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">In May, the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) said data centers that followed the rules did not have to stop using the chips they had, until further notice.<Cite c="may-31-tolerance" /> The rule ends that: a data center already serving Chinese companies at that scale has {n.graceDays} days to get a license or face future export restrictions.<Cite c="existing-90-days" /> It also reaches chips already installed: a data center that starts serving Chinese companies above the threshold after its chips arrive needs a license for that change, unless the computing is used only to recommend products or content to users and is reported to BIS.<Cite c="installed-chips" /><Cite c="data-center-license" /> The rule took effect November 19, and BIS takes public comments on the rule until January 19, 2027.<Cite c="effective-on-publication" /><Cite c="comments-60-days" /> Commerce's announcement says Treasury and the White House National Security Council wrote it.<Cite c="written-by-treasury-nsc" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="how-it-got-here">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">A rule promised for 18 months</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">BIS says the rule is meant to stop companies from getting around existing limits on American technology by renting computing power at data centers in other countries.<Cite c="bis-purpose" /> In January 2025, President Biden's administration issued the AI Diffusion Rule, which included limits on Chinese firms' remote access to data centers and was due to take effect that May. Trump's Commerce Department dropped it before it was ever enforced and promised a replacement.<Cite c="biden-rule-rescinded" /><Cite c="replacement-promised" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Top officials pressed in October for rules restricting Chinese access to computing power in Southeast Asia, and the president signed off, according to people who described the talks to The New York Times. Howard Lutnick, the Commerce secretary, said he did not understand the rush and warned the step would set off a further trade war with China, those people said.<Cite c="october-signoff" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Megaspeed, a Singapore company, had been building data centers in Southeast Asia to serve Chinese clients, and U.S. authorities are investigating it over alleged chip smuggling.<Cite c="megaspeed-serves-china" /> On November 12, Nvidia's chief executive, Jensen Huang, stepped down after reports of his affair with Alice Huang, a former Megaspeed executive. U.S. authorities deemed her an asset of Chinese intelligence, The New York Times reports; China denies she is a spy, and Singapore, which investigated Megaspeed, says it did not investigate her for espionage.<Cite c="megaspeed-scandal" /><Cite c="singapore-huang" /> The next day Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent blamed Commerce for not putting Megaspeed on the entity list, a trade blacklist, and Sens. Lisa Blunt Rochester, a Delaware Democrat, and John Curtis, a Utah Republican, wrote to Lutnick urging limits on remote access, the renting of chips over a network.<Cite c="bessent-has-the-pen" /><Cite c="blunt-rochester-curtis" /> The rule came three days later.<Cite c="rule-issued" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the 2022 limits to the rule</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="who-wrote-it">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who wrote it</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The rule is a Commerce rule, signed by Jeffrey Kessler, the department's under secretary for export controls.<Cite c="kessler-signed" /> But Commerce's announcement says it was "written by and at the direction of the Department of Treasury and the National Security Council."<Cite c="written-by-treasury-nsc" /> Three people told Politico that Bessent pushed it through, with the support of Marco Rubio, the secretary of state and national security adviser.<Cite c="bessent-pushed" /> Lutnick had wanted a leaner rule, one less onerous for Nvidia and its customers, the Times reported.<Cite c="lutnick-leaner" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Chris McGuire, Council on Foreign Relations" when="November 16, 2026" where="Post on X">
          <p>"I have never seen a BIS rule that was not written by BIS."<Cite c="mcguire-unprecedented" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A Commerce spokeswoman said the department was already considering remote-access rules with other agencies, and on November 17 Commerce and the Justice Department said Commerce had been investigating Megaspeed since 2024.<Cite c="commerce-considering" /><Cite c="commerce-megaspeed-statement" /> Trump said there were "two different views," and that Lutnick might have delayed action because otherwise "you could have had a blowup with China."<Cite c="trump-two-views" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>A license, not a ban</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The rule requires a license; it bans nothing outright. Data-center licenses are reviewed case by case, and the new requirement covers a data center that gives Chinese companies more than {n.threshold} TPP.<Cite c="case-by-case" /><Cite c="below-threshold" /><Cite c="d5-includes-china" /> NBC's story said remote access "will be banned."<Cite c="nbc-banned" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The rule controls chips, not computing: the license is needed to ship chips to a data center serving Chinese companies above the threshold, and the rule's summary describes those as overseas data centers.<Cite c="abroad-only" /> Below the threshold, it requires no license unless BIS tells a company one is needed.<Cite c="below-threshold" /> Separately, BIS says an older control already requires a license when a Chinese company is a data center's main user.<Cite c="below-threshold" /> Most chips meant for a Chinese-headquartered company itself face a presumption of denial: BIS starts from refusing them.<Cite c="presumption-of-denial" /> BIS expects about {n.licenses} more licenses a year.<Cite c="about-100-licenses" /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <figcaption><strong>What the coverage said, and what the rule says</strong></figcaption>
                <table class="data-table catch-compare">
                  <thead><tr><th>Coverage said</th><th>The rule says</th></tr></thead>
                  <tbody>{headlinesVsRule.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.said}<Cite c={r.saidC} /></td><td>{r.rule}{r.ruleC.map((id) => <Cite c={id} />)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
                </table>
              </figure>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Who wanted it to go further</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">McGuire calls the rule's silence on U.S. data centers its biggest gap: Chinese companies, he says, can still legally train on advanced chips "at data centers in Northern Virginia."<Cite c="us-data-centers-gap" /> Blunt Rochester and Curtis had asked for limits that are not geographically bound, warning that China would otherwise rent chips elsewhere, including through U.S. subsidiaries.<Cite c="blunt-rochester-curtis" /> The House passed a bill in January, the Remote Access Security Act, that would extend export controls to remote access to U.S. items by foreign persons; it awaits the Senate.<Cite c="rasa" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="explanations">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Every explanation, tested</h2>
        <ExplanationList entries={explanations} />
      </section>

      <section id="coverage">
        <h2 data-layer="fact" data-claims="ap-immediately nyt-90-days wsj-proposed effective-on-publication existing-90-days not-a-proposal">Three reports got the start date or the rule's status wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The Associated Press</strong> wrote that BIS issued "the rule that took effect immediately."<Cite c="ap-immediately" /> It took effect on November 19, when the Federal Register published it.<Cite c="effective-on-publication" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The New York Times</strong> wrote that the provision "goes into effect in 90 days."<Cite c="nyt-90-days" /> The 90 days is the deadline for data centers already serving Chinese firms at that scale to get a license; the rule itself took effect on publication, November 19.<Cite c="existing-90-days" /><Cite c="effective-on-publication" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The Wall Street Journal</strong>, in an early version of its story on November 16, called it a "new proposed rule"; a later version that day did not.<Cite c="wsj-proposed" /> It is an interim final rule, which the export control law lets BIS issue without first taking comment.<Cite c="not-a-proposal" /> It took effect on publication, November 19, and BIS takes comments until January 19, 2027.<Cite c="effective-on-publication" /><Cite c="comments-60-days" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The dates ahead</strong></figcaption>
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{steps.map((s) => <tr><td>{s.when}</td><td>{s.what}{s.c.map((id) => <Cite c={id} />)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">China's commerce ministry says the rule departs from the understanding Trump and Xi reached and that China will take countermeasures if the United States persists.<Cite c="china-countermeasures" /> Singapore says it is talking to U.S. officials about access for its companies.<Cite c="singapore-trusted-access" /> Nvidia's board set up a committee on national security and export controls.<Cite c="nvidia-committee" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .story-figure .data-table td, .story-figure .data-table th { word-break: normal; overflow-wrap: normal; hyphens: none; }
  .catch-compare th:first-child, .catch-compare td:first-child { width: 34%; }
</style>
